WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.167
Closed: calls flow. The breaker allows normal calls and records eligible outcomes. Timeouts still bound each attempt; a closed breaker is not a guarantee of health.

2
00:00:12.167 --> 00:00:23.000
Observe repeated failures. The dependency times out repeatedly. A minimum sample and measurement window help distinguish a pattern from a single unlucky request.

3
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:34.917
Open: stop pressure. The breaker opens and rejects dependency calls quickly. This frees local resources and avoids repeating expensive failures while recovery is investigated.

4
00:00:34.917 --> 00:00:47.167
Choose an honest fallback. Optional recommendations can be omitted. Essential payment outcomes must remain pending or failed according to verified state, not invented to hide the outage.

5
00:00:47.167 --> 00:00:58.333
Half-open: probe. After a reset interval, allow a limited probe set. Sending every waiting caller at once can overload a dependency that is only partly recovered.

6
00:00:58.333 --> 00:01:09.833
Recover or reopen. Successful probes restore normal traffic; failed probes reopen the breaker. Observe both false trips and recovery behavior under controlled failure tests.
